  [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mC. Identity store plus a login event and a separate tracking subscriber[0m
  As A, but [38;5;153mlogin[39m dispatches [38;5;153mCustomEvent('auth:login', { detail: { userId, correlationId } })[39m and a separate [38;5;153mtracking.js
[39m  subscribes. Tracking's destination can change without touching login.
  [3mTrades:[0m the cleanest separation between "who is logged in" and "what we do about it", and it enforces the
  correlation-vs-auth split in the payload. But it's an extra file and a layer of indirection for an app whose tracking
  is currently one [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m. Over-built unless tracking becomes real.
  [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mI recommend A.[0m The repo has no build, no dev server, no tests, no dependencies — A is the only option that doesn't
  change how you develop. More importantly, the expensive-to-reverse decisions here are the [3mstorage[0m ones (which keys,
  which store, what clears when), and A centralizes those identically to B and C. Module format and eventing can both be
  retrofitted later without redoing them; that makes A the YAGNI-correct starting point rather than a corner.

  Two things I'd flag before you pick:

  - [1mC's value hinges on a question I haven't asked[0m — where tracking actually sends data. If the answer is "console for
    now, an analytics endpoint later," C earns its keep. If it's "console, permanently," C is scaffolding for nothing.
    I'll make that section 2 of the design.
  - [1mWith no server, [38;5;153mlogin[39m has to invent the auth id.[0m It'll be a generated value marked explicitly as a placeholder,
    sitting exactly where a server-issued id will later land. That's the seam.
